### Further checks

- **usc2_conservative:** 157/157 comparable ignition cases and 68/68 comparable flames; maximum secondary ignition error 5.91%; maximum burned-temperature difference 0.90 K. On six representative cases each, tightening ignition tolerances changes delay by at most 1.12e-06%; refining flames from slope 0.03 to 0.015 changes speed by at most 0.87% and changes reduced-versus-parent error by at most 0.10 percentage points.
- **usc2_balanced_repaired:** 157/157 comparable ignition cases and 68/68 comparable flames; maximum secondary ignition error 12.17%; maximum burned-temperature difference 0.95 K. On six representative cases each, tightening ignition tolerances changes delay by at most 1.07e-05%; refining flames from slope 0.03 to 0.015 changes speed by at most 0.87% and changes reduced-versus-parent error by at most 0.36 percentage points.
- **usc2_compact_repaired:** 157/157 comparable ignition cases and 68/68 comparable flames; maximum secondary ignition error 13.96%; maximum burned-temperature difference 1.29 K. On six representative cases each, tightening ignition tolerances changes delay by at most 5.55e-06%; refining flames from slope 0.03 to 0.015 changes speed by at most 0.88% and changes reduced-versus-parent error by at most 0.24 percentage points.

The first parent grid study found up to **2.615%** change between slope 0.06 and 0.015. This prompted refinement of **every final parent and reduced flame** to slope 0.03; further representative refinement changed parent speeds by at most about 0.86%. These are numerical sensitivity checks, not independently certified error bounds. See [numerical results](results/numerical_comparison.json).

## External experimental comparison

Burcat, Snyder and Brabbs, **NASA TM-87312 (1986)**, Table III mixture A, report 12 toluene/O2/Ar shocks at 1358–1758 K and 2.33–3.61 atm. The mole fractions are 0.01495 toluene, 0.1345 oxygen and 0.85055 argon. Their values were checked against the [original public report](https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19860015959/downloads/19860015959.pdf), which is also [saved locally](sources/burcat_original.pdf).

The primary report defines ignition as the **beginning of pressure rise**. The public ChemKED transcription instead labels it as maximum dP/dt. Accordingly, we report both the simulated pressure-derivative peak and a stated **onset estimate**: the initial-pressure intercept of the tangent at maximum dP/dt. This estimate is not an exact model of the experimental onset selection. The transcription’s 0.1 microsecond value was not treated as a complete experimental uncertainty; the original text associates that timing precision with shock-speed measurement.

| Model | Mean absolute onset-estimate error | Maximum onset-estimate error | Mean / maximum pressure-peak error |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Parent | 36.14% | 56.37% | 38.52% / 68.61% |
| usc2_conservative | 37.13% | 57.21% | 39.51% / 69.43% |
| usc2_balanced_repaired | 30.03% | 48.54% | 32.48% / 60.99% |
| usc2_compact_repaired | 28.33% | 44.06% | 31.01% / 57.74% |

The parent overpredicts all 12 observed delays using the onset estimate. Its systematic discrepancy is inherited to varying degrees by the reduced mechanisms. Reduction fidelity therefore **does not imply experimental accuracy at the 5–10% level**. These observations were not used to fit rates or select species. There is **no direct flame-speed experimental validation** in this run; the flame comparisons are parent predictions of an experimentally measurable macroscopic observable.

